content.text:
Yes, syntactically:

```ts
protected showGlobalBack: boolean = (() => {
  const mode = this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_backward_button;

  switch (mode) {
    case 'HIDDEN':
    case undefined:
      return false;
    case 'DYNAMIC':
    case 'UNITS':
    case 'PAGES':
      return true;
    default: {
      const exhaustiveCheck: never = mode;
      return exhaustiveCheck;
    }
  }
})();
```

But I would not. Field init runs once, during component construction. If `tcs.booklet` is loaded later, `showGlobalBack` stays `false`.

Better:

```ts
protected get showGlobalBack(): boolean {
  const mode = this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_backward_button;
  return mode === 'DYNAMIC' || mode === 'UNITS' || mode === 'PAGES';
}
```

Use current helper if you want exhaustive checking; use getter if value must track booklet changes.

Yes. Runtime immutability does not help here because the issue is init order.

`showGlobalBack = this.deriveGlobalBack()` snapshots too early if `tcs.booklet` is still `undefined`.

Use either:

```ts
protected get showGlobalBack(): boolean {
  return this.deriveGlobalBack();
}
```

or assign after booklet parse/load completes:

```ts
this.showGlobalBack = this.deriveGlobalBack();
```

The getter is minimal and avoids hunting exact load timing. Cost is negligible here.

Yes: in `ngOnInit`, inside route subscription, directly after:

```ts
await this.tls.loadTest();
```

That is the first point where `TestLoaderService` has parsed and assigned `this.tcs.booklet`.

Example:

```ts
protected showGlobalBack = false;
```

```ts
await this.tls.loadTest();
this.showGlobalBack = this.deriveGlobalBack();
this.testLoaded = true;
```

So caller should be `TestControllerComponent`, not `TestControllerService`. Reason: `showGlobalBack` is view state for this component. `TestLoaderService` should load data, not know which buttons this component caches.

But for this exact case, getter is still cleaner:

```ts
protected get showGlobalBack(): boolean {
  return this.deriveGlobalBack();
}
```

Because template already gates with `testLoaded`, so it only matters after load.

Yes. In Angular templates, a getter used like:

```html
@if (showGlobalBack && testLoaded) {
```

is evaluated during each change detection pass.

So yes, it can be called often. For this getter, cost is tiny: one optional chain + one switch. Fine.

If you want zero repeated evaluation, assign once after load:

```ts
protected showGlobalBack = false;
```

```ts
await this.tls.loadTest();
this.showGlobalBack = this.deriveGlobalBack();
this.testLoaded = true;
```

Given this value is immutable after booklet load, I’d use assignment after `loadTest()`. It matches the model best and avoids template getter debate.

Yes. `@if (...)` expressions are part of Angular change detection too.

So this:

```html
@if (showBack === true) {
```

re-reads `showBack` every change detection pass.

If `showBack` is a getter:

```ts
get showBack(): boolean {
  return this.deriveGlobalBack();
}
```

Angular calls the getter every change detection pass.

Performance impact depends on getter cost:

```ts
protected get showGlobalBack(): boolean {
  const mode = this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_backward_button;
  return mode === 'DYNAMIC' || mode === 'UNITS' || mode === 'PAGES';
}
```

This is negligible. Not worth optimizing unless the getter does loops, allocations, filtering, sorting, service calls, or changes state.

Still, for your case, because booklet config is fixed after load, a plain assigned boolean is semantically best:

```ts
await this.tls.loadTest();
this.showGlobalBack = this.deriveGlobalBack();
this.testLoaded = true;
```
